    "language_probability": 1,
    "text": "Then in the nineteen sixties the cracks appear. America is spending heavily on the war in Vietnam and on huge programs at home. It creates far more dollars than it has gold to back them. Those dollars pile up in banks across Europe and some governments start to get nervous. France, under President Charles de Gaulle, begins trading its dollars for American gold and shipping that gold home. Others quietly follow. Between the late nineteen fifties and nineteen seventy one, America's gold reserves fall by more than half. France's finance minister, Valery Giscard d'Estaing, had a name for what America enjoyed, an exorbitant privilege, the power to pay for almost anything in a currency that only America can print.",
